\paragraph{Pretrained vs.\ finetuned: degeneracy as the mechanism.}
On CoNLL, the pretrained model achieves comparable $\rho_{\textup{SJ}}$ ($0.47$ vs.\ $0.41$--$0.45$ finetuned) but MV captures ${\sim}98$\% of oracle headroom (degeneracy $36$\%) versus ${<}2$\% finetuned (degeneracy $57$--$60$\%).
Fine-tuning decouples agreement from quality through dataset-dependent pathways---diversity elimination on CoNLL, correlated error patterns on Few-NERD (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}); per-type analysis confirms 7/8 entity types flip MV direction from positive to negative after finetuning (Appendix~\ref{app:pertype}).

\paragraph{From diagnosis to practice.}\label{sec:selection_method}
Entity-level construction bypasses both failure mechanisms when degeneracy ${<}22$\% ($p{<}0.01$; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}); the trade-off ($N{\times}$ cost for ${\approx}1$\,pp) may only justify safety-critical use.
LP compression (median 0.041 nats) connects to calibration challenges \cite{kadavath2022language, xiong2024verbalized}.
We position these gains ($+0.3$--$1.4$\,pp at $N{\times}$ inference cost) as diagnostic evidence rather than practical recommendations.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}\label{sec:conclusion}
NER exhibits oracle scaling ($c{\approx}0.5$) but aggregation fails above $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$; entity construction and LP selection bypass this barrier at entity granularity.
A major contributing factor is near-zero selection alignment ($\alpha{\to}0$) even when $\rho$ is substantial.

